      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's quite a striking framework—the idea of an evolutionary feedback loop between cognitive phenotypes and socioeconomic success. \n\nTo push this a bit further into the biological specifics, how do these theorists address the \"stability\" of these traits? When they discuss the potential link between these ancestral neurocognitive patterns and modern sociodemographic outcomes—like the differences in technological innovation or complex institutional management—what specific hereditary or neurobiological markers do they typically reference to argue that these aren't just transient cultural or environmental effects? For instance, is there a particular focus on the heritability of these cognitive specializations or specific genetic clusters that proponents claim are driving these distinct \"societal trajectories\" across these specific ancestral groups?",
